The one surviving comparative project
Atezolizumab/Bevacizumab vs STRIDE as first-line therapy — 51 vs 41 patients from 2023-04-21, index-year SMD 0.043, all six gates pass (research-protocol share in the STRIDE arm, 25%, is the one marginal item).

v9 reached a similar recommendation by an invalid route: it reported SMD 0.03 across all 144 vs 80 episodes and then blocked the node on positivity. On the usable non-research subset the unrestricted SMD is −0.977 — severe imbalance, because STRIDE's early adopters are trial patients. Calendar balance exists only inside the overlap window, and inside that window positivity passes.

Top three non-comparative projects (immune to the equipoise failures)
Dynamic ALBI trajectory on systemic therapy — 585 episodes with ≥3 on-treatment measurements (median 6); within-patient design, so the date shift is exact. Strongest available project.
AFP kinetics as an early response signal — 542 episodes with baseline plus on-treatment AFP. Newly unlocked by correction 3.
Discontinuation reasons and consequences — 92.7% recorded, 82.4% informative; time-to-discontinuation is shift-invariant.
Date-shift framework
Every patient's dates move by one fixed offset ≤ 6 months, so within-patient intervals are exact (verified: 0 days of drift across 50 jitter draws) while absolute calendar claims are bounded. Episodes are classified DEFINITELY_PRE / DATE_SHIFT_UNCERTAIN / DEFINITELY_POST against each approval anchor; 88–90% land in a definite category and the 10–12% uncertain band is unresolvable by any method and must be reported as its own stratum.

